{
  "dom_id": "step-q6900",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(0,10)$ and $(4,26).$",
  "worked_solution": "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{26-10}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{4} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,10),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=10.$ \n\nSubstituting the slope $m=4$ and the $y$-intercept $b=10$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=4x+10.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
